<h2>Feeding Vietnam's food-processing and aquaculture sectors</h2>
<p>Vietnam's Indian import profile centres on inputs for two of its largest industries: fish meal for its substantial aquaculture sector, and groundnut kernels for food and oil processing. Dried red chilli adds further volume.</p>

<h2>Aquaculture demand is the story behind fish meal</h2>
<p>Vietnam is one of the world's largest producers of farmed shrimp and pangasius catfish, and that industry needs a steady, quality-consistent feed-ingredient supply. Fish meal specification — protein content, freshness indicators, contamination testing — matters more to Vietnamese feed-mill buyers than to most markets, so expect detailed technical questions on a first enquiry.</p>

<h2>India as a complementary, not competing, supplier</h2>
<p>Vietnam is itself a major agricultural exporter, so where India supplies Vietnam it's usually because Indian origin offers something specific — price, available volume, or timing that domestic or other regional supply doesn't cover at that moment. That's worth keeping in mind when planning a Vietnam-facing sourcing programme: continuity of supply may matter as much as price.</p>

